      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for catching the problem, Charan.\n\nI am curious whether this is general problem or not between page_ext and offline.\n\nCPU A is accessing random page descriptor(so access page_ext naturally since page_ext is extension of struct page) but CPU B is offlining and then free the page_ext?\n\nA example,\n\npage_idle_bitmap_read                      \n  page_is_idle                          free_ext\n     lookup_page_ext\n",
      "revId": "05c63911b098b1d5d8f2305c36b44096575aa398",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4688fd6b_ae032a46",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1904204
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-17T16:17:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As per my understanding, As long as there is an extra refcount on the page, or the page_ext being accessed in the path where the final put_page() happens, it will be fine. Because extra ref_count makes the offline to wait till the refcount is zero or in the final put_page() where page_ext access happens(say for page_owner) before it is freed to buddy. \n\nWith simple grep, I feel that all paths are seems fine(unless I am really missing something here), but one where the reading of /proc/page_owner information directly and there is a parallel offlining happening.\n\n/proc/page_owner                  offline\n----------------               ------------\nPageBuddy(page) returns\nfalse\n                              Frees the page to buddy\n                              and successively free the\n                              ms-\u003epage_ext\nAccess the page_ext\n\nI think there should be extra refcount of the page should be taken here.\n\nBTW, for the use case you have mentioned, page_is_idle() is just a page-\u003eflags check for 64bit. It is for 32 bit where we do page_ext-\u003eflags but memory hotplug is not enabled for 32bit systems,(If I am not wrong here), to online/offline memory blocks.",
      "parentUuid": "8169d5d6_b9fca3da",
      "revId": "05c63911b098b1d5d8f2305c36b44096575aa398",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ddabdc1a_c788098e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1275554
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-17T16:44:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I didn\u0027t know memory_hotplug works for only 64 bit. Thanks.\n\nI knew why page_is_idle uses page_ext only when 64bit because of lacking of page-\u003eflags. IOW, If we don\u0027t see the crash there, it\u0027s coincidence.\n\nAbout put_page with offlining explanation above, I agree with your analysis.\n\nHowever, I am talking about more of design problem: page, page_ext and memory hotplugging.\n\nTo me, it\u0027s rather strange that page_ext and page have different life cycle even though one is extension of the other. I am not familiar with hotplugging. But isn\u0027t it also free struct page memmap area in the end? And kernel can access any struct page descriptor speculatively? Then, how does hotplug prevent the crash?\n\nshouldn\u0027t we make page_ext freeing after the struct page in generall since page_ext is just extension of struct page? Thus, user should access page_ext via page and if the page descriptor is already freed, they couldn\u0027t access page_ext, either. And I hope memory hotplug already has some way to prevent accessing struct page before freeing.\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "4688fd6b_ae032a46",
      "revId": "05c63911b098b1d5d8f2305c36b44096575aa398",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0223cc86_c8652075",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1904204
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-17T19:41:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e\u003eI didn\u0027t know memory_hotplug works for only 64 bit.\nI should be correcting this statement by saying that I had mentioned it only for ARM architecture where we are doing it only for 64bit. Not really have an idea about other architectures. But a simple grep showing that it seems there for x86 32bit with HIGHMEM. In such cases, your example about page_is_idle() is also a valid one.\n\n\u003e\u003e But isn\u0027t it also free struct page memmap area in the end? ......\nOfflining a memory block won\u0027t free the memmap but removal of memory block does it. And I am really doubt If kernel can access any page descriptor speculatively once memmap is removed, The offlining steps will be like \n1) Isolate all the pages \n2) while(1); try free the pages to buddy.( Here I mean to -\u003efree_list[MIGRATE_ISOLATE])\n3) delete the pages from this buddy list. \n4) Then free page_ext.  \n\nWith the step 1) above, kernel can\u0027t really allocates the pages any more\n2) only can succeed with the final put_page() from any path(which again makes the page directly goes to isolated free list). \n3) Is making the pages to be completely removed from the buddy. \nSo, I see that kernel can no way speculatively access page descriptor after offlining itself. After this If user wants, he can remove the page block which then removes the \u0027memmap\u0027.\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003et\u0027s rather strange that page_ext and page have different life cycle ......\nAnd I should agree with the design problem here about page, page_ext and its freeing in memory_hotplug. In theory, as long as page is accessible, the page_ext also should be accessible, but ***Why one should be needing the access to page_ext, If the page it self is not part of any buddy list? IOW, why one need to make checks, like page_is_idle(), on the page that isn\u0027t even a valid one***. Isn\u0027t the expectation of page_ext presence only when the page is onlined is suffice?",
      "parentUuid": "ddabdc1a_c788098e",
      "revId": "05c63911b098b1d5d8f2305c36b44096575aa398",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
